# Intent: A small face glyph in the banner status strip, next to the microphone glyph, shows while a face was seen in the last 3 seconds, only while the Status icons switch is on (UI04 rules). It is its own strip slot, so it never replaces or covers the camera cue, the remote chip, the privacy cues or the battery and Wi-Fi glyphs. New unit tool self.banner.face_seen {seconds} (default 3, at most 10) sets the glyph; reports closer than half a second apart are ignored, so the host and the unit's own detector (FD patches, which call the same internal function when they find a face) may both report. The glyph and its timing rules live in maipai_status_core.h and maipai_banner_core.h (MB_FLAG_FACE sits right after MB_FLAG_MIC), the drawing in maipai_banner.cc.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_status_core.h, maipai_banner_core.h, maipai_banner.h, maipai_banner.cc, maipai_banner_tools.cc, hal_mcp.cpp, maipai_facedet.cc (ours)
# Check after a flash: with the Status icons switch on (self.prefs.set status_icons on), self.banner.face_seen {"seconds":3} shows a small face glyph next to the mic glyph that goes by itself after 3 s; with the switch off the answer says shown false and nothing is drawn.
# Kconfig: none

